    { "@type": "Question", "name": "What is DeepSeek V4.1 Flash and when was it released?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "DeepSeek V4.1 Flash is a multimodal Mixture-of-Experts model with 552B backbone parameters, released on September 10, 2026. It is the smallest member of DeepSeek's new model-structure family and replaces both DeepSeek-V4-Flash and the experimental DeepSeek-V4-Flash-Vision-Exp. The model natively processes images and text and supports contexts up to one million tokens. In the DeepSeek API the callable model name is now simply deepseek-flash; the legacy names deepseek-v4-flash and deepseek-v4-flash-vision-exp still resolve but are routed to V4.1 Flash and billed at V4.1 Flash rates." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "How much does DeepSeek V4.1 Flash cost per million tokens?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "DeepSeek prices V4.1 Flash in Chinese yuan with a peak/off-peak split. Off-peak: 0.02 CNY per million cache-hit input tokens, 1 CNY per million cache-miss input tokens, and 4 CNY per million output tokens. Peak: 0.04 CNY cache-hit input, 2 CNY cache-miss input, and 8 CNY output. Peak hours are Beijing time Monday to Friday 09:00-12:00 and 14:00-18:00; everything else is off-peak, which costs exactly half. On OpenRouter the model lists at 0.30 USD per million input tokens, 1.20 USD per million output tokens, and 0.006 USD per million cache-read tokens." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Why is the KV cache size of DeepSeek V4.1 Flash important?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "DeepSeek reports a global KV cache footprint of 890 bytes per token for V4.1 Flash, roughly one quarter of DeepSeek-V4-Flash and about 437x smaller than the original DeepSeek-V1 generation. Because a one-million-token agent session must keep its key-value state resident in memory while the model works, a four-fold smaller cache is a direct reduction in the GPU memory a long-running agent needs - and GPU memory is what determines how many concurrent sessions a given cluster can serve. DeepSeek achieves this with FP4 main KV caching in E2M1 format, Compressed Sparse Attention 2 (CSA2) layer sharing, and SWA Bounded Replay, which cuts the persistent KV footprint to about one eighth of V4 Flash." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "What happens to DeepSeek V4 Pro on September 14, 2026?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "DeepSeek is retiring the deepseek-v4-pro model. From 12:00 Beijing time on September 14, 2026 until a future V4.1 Pro ships, any request that specifies deepseek-v4-pro will be routed to V4.1 Flash and billed at V4.1 Flash prices. DeepSeek's stated reason is that, across multiple test rounds, V4.1 Flash has comprehensively surpassed V4 Pro on performance, cost, speed, and total time-to-completion. Teams with production code pinned to deepseek-v4-pro should treat this as a forced migration date, not a deprecation notice - the model ID keeps working but the underlying model and the price both change." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Is DeepSeek V4.1 Flash open source?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. The weights are published on Hugging Face under the MIT license at deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash, alongside a full technical report and an evaluation folder with reproduction instructions for the DeepSWE v1.1 benchmark. The model card describes a 40-layer Transformer split into a 20-layer causal encoder and a 20-layer decoder, 384 routed experts plus 1 shared expert per MoE layer, 6 routed experts activated per token, and pre-training on a 45-trillion-token multimodal corpus. The config also declares an Engram conditional memory block of 196B parameters and DSpark speculative decoding." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Should long-context agent builders switch to DeepSeek V4.1 Flash?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "The economics favor switching if your workload is input-heavy or agentic. Activating 8B parameters per token during prefill and 16B during decode, combined with an off-peak cache-miss input rate near one fifteenth of V4 Pro's and a cache-hit rate near one fourteenth, makes V4.1 Flash dramatically cheaper for long documents with reused prefixes. The trade-off is raw depth: on HLE it scores 36.8 against Opus-5.0's 56.3 and GPT-5.6 Sol's 44.5, and its ExploitGym score of 15.3 trails both frontier models. Treat it as a high-throughput workhorse and route your hardest reasoning steps elsewhere rather than assuming the cheaper model closes every gap." } }
